    10	## Validation at execute time
    11	
    12	- When `input` omits a property listed in `required`, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property as required and never invokes the executor ([validated by: rejects an input missing a required property before invoking the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L35), [validated by: rejects input missing a required property with a ToolResponse and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L16)).
    13	- When `input` supplies a property whose value does not match its declared JSON Schema type, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` naming the failing field path and expected type without invoking the executor, and neither `result` nor the emitted log record contains the offending property value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property naming the field and expected type without leaking the offending value to the result or logs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L46)).
    14	- When `input` violates an `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` identifies the field path and `enum` as the failed constraint without invoking the executor ([validated by: identifies enum as the failed constraint without invoking the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L114)).
    15	- A schema rejection is logged with its tool name and the failed property path and constraint, but never the invalid input value ([validated by: logs a schema rejection without the invalid input value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L59)).
    16	- When `input` satisfies `required`, declared property types and `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` invokes the executor once and passes the input object through unchanged — including undeclared extra properties — without injecting `additionalProperties: false`, coercing types or applying defaults ([validated by: passes valid input and undeclared extra properties to the executor unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L86)).
    17	- When `ToolRegistry.execute` is called with a tool name that has not been registered, it throws `Unknown tool: <name>` because an unregistered tool is a programming or configuration error rather than a recoverable input mistake.
